maria_g: Heads up — hitting weird import errors when trying to use the JS SDK inside an edge function (Vercel edge runtime). docs show `import` examples but our sample app used `require()` and it fails with SyntaxError.

maria_g: Minimal repro:
```js
// edge handler
const { createClient } = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
// throws SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'
```

jonathan: yeah that usually means the package is shipping ESM and CJS `require()` can't parse it. Edge runtimes on Vercel/Cloudflare expect ESM. Do you have `type: 'module'` in package.json?

maria_g: package.json is CJS (no type field). We couldn't switch project-wide to ESM. Any suggestions short-term?

kevin_s: short term options:
- use dynamic import: `const { createClient } = await import('@redwood/inference');` inside an async handler (works in many runtimes)
- or use a tiny wrapper that re-exports CJS: `export { default as createClient } from '@redwood/inference';` and compile to CJS for your app
- or pin to a CJS-dist if we publish one (not sure we do)

maria_g: dynamic import worked for me in dev but worried about perf on cold start. Also TS typings disappeared when using dynamic import and `--esModuleInterop` false.

elaine: Oof. This lines up with a few support tickets. We have two separate docs issues:
1) Quickstart only shows `import` (ESM) examples, no `require()` or dynamic import guidance.
2) Typings edge-cases when people mix `esModuleInterop` false + CJS projects.

jonathan: For typings: if the package exports a default in ESM (`export default`) but TS consumers use `const sdk = require('@redwood/inference')`, TS inference breaks unless `esModuleInterop` or `allowSyntheticDefaultImports` is enabled. We should call that out in our compatibility section.

kevin_s: Also worth noting: bundlers like esbuild/webpack may rewrite `require()` differently. For edge targets we should recommend ESM-first examples and a snippet for the common CJS fallback (dynamic import).

maria_g: Can we add a tiny snippet to the docs showing:
```js
// CJS app, edge runtime workaround
module.exports = async (req, res) => {
  const { createClient } = await import('@redwood/inference');
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
  res.send(await client.generate('hello'));
};
```

elaine: good idea. I'll add a short section called "Using the JS SDK in CJS projects / Edge runtimes" to the quickstart. Also add a note about TS flags (`esModuleInterop`) and a link to the bundler caveats.

maria_g: thanks! also can we add a snippet for named vs default export confusion? like `import sdk from` vs `import { createClient } from` — people keep trying the wrong form and hitting undefined.

kevin_s: yes. Our package currently exposes named `createClient` and also a default that is an alias for convenience. Docs should show both forms explicitly and explain when each is needed. Eg:
```js
// ESM named
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference';
// ESM default
import createClientDefault from '@redwood/inference';
// CJS
const { createClient } = require('@redwood/inference'); // fails if package is ESM-only
```

elaine: I'll draft the PR. Assigning to @elaine. Will include: short explanation, code examples (ESM, CJS wrapper, dynamic import), TS compatibility note, and a sentence about runtime perf tradeoffs for dynamic import.

docs-bot: PR created: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/412 (WIP) :sparkles:

maria_g: awesome, thanks. once merged i'll try it in our app and report back if any remaining issues. :thumbsup:
